const q=(title,context,options,feedback,hint)=>({title,context,options,correct:0,feedback,hint});
const lesson=(name,group,lab,title,intro,experiment,discovery,prompt,notes,questions,extra={})=>({name,group,lab,title,intro,experiment,discovery,prompt,notesTitle:name,notes,questions,time:'~12 MINUTES',controls:'Change one thing',...extra});
export const advancedLessons={
matrices:lesson('Matrix arithmetic','01 · Foundations','arithmetic','An array of numbers.<br>A language for structure.','Read a matrix by its shape, then explore addition, multiplication, and transpose. The order of a product matters.','When does A × B differ from B × A?','Matrix multiplication combines rows of the first matrix with columns of the second. It is composition of transformations, not element-by-element multiplication.','Compare A × B and B × A. Then transpose A: what happened to its rows?',
`<p>An <strong>m × n matrix</strong> has m rows and n columns. It can store data or represent a linear map from ℝⁿ to ℝᵐ. The entry in row i, column j is written aᵢⱼ.</p><h3>Add corresponding entries</h3><p>Addition requires the same shape. Scalar multiplication multiplies every entry. For matrices A and B of the same shape, A + B = B + A.</p><h3>Multiply rows by columns</h3><div class="math">(AB)ᵢⱼ = Σₖ aᵢₖ bₖⱼ<br>(m × n)(n × p) → m × p</div><p>For square matrices, AB means apply B first, then A. Reversing that order generally changes the result. For example, a horizontal stretch followed by a shear differs from the shear followed by the stretch.</p><h3>Transpose and identity</h3><p>The transpose Aᵀ exchanges rows and columns. For real matrices, (AB)ᵀ = BᵀAᵀ. The identity I leaves vectors unchanged: AI = IA = A. An inverse A⁻¹ exists only for square matrices of full rank.</p><p>Our visual experiments use 2 × 2 matrices. The shape rules and definitions apply to rectangular matrices too. Try multiplying a 2 × 3 matrix by a 3 × 1 matrix on paper: what shape must the answer have?</p>`,[
q('Check the shape before the arithmetic.','A has shape 2 × 3 and B has shape 3 × 4. What shape is AB?',['2 × 4','3 × 3','The product is undefined.'],['Correct. The inner dimensions match; the outer dimensions give the output shape.','The inner dimension is summed over. It does not determine the output shape.','The matching inner dimensions, 3 and 3, make this product valid.'],'Read (m × n)(n × p) → m × p.'),
q('Order is part of the operation.','A stretches horizontally; B shears horizontally. Which claim is generally true?',['AB and BA can differ.','AB always equals BA.','AB means applying A first, then B.'],['Yes. A composition generally depends on its order.','Addition commutes; matrix multiplication generally does not.','ABv = A(Bv), so B acts first.'],'Apply each product to the vector (0, 1).')],{prereq:['vectors'],initial:{m:[2,0,0,1],n:[1,1,0,1],operation:'ab'}}),
elimination:lesson('Gaussian elimination','02 · Systems & spaces','elimination','Simplify the equations.<br>Keep the same answers.','Use exact row operations to turn a system into a form you can read. Each legal move preserves its solution set.','Your next row operation','A row operation must act on the whole augmented row, including the right-hand side. Multiplying a row by zero is not reversible.','Start with R₂ ← R₂ − 2R₁. What entry disappears? Then look for the next pivot.',
`<p>A system Ax = b becomes an <strong>augmented matrix</strong> [A | b]. Each row represents an equation. The vertical separator distinguishes coefficients from the right-hand side.</p><h3>Three reversible moves</h3><ul><li>Swap two rows.</li><li>Multiply a row by a nonzero scalar.</li><li>Add a multiple of one row to a different row.</li></ul><p>Apply every move to the entire row. These operations preserve exactly which vectors solve the system. Scaling by zero destroys information and is not a legal elementary row operation.</p><h3>Find pivots, then eliminate</h3><p>A pivot is the leading nonzero entry of a nonzero row. In echelon form, pivots move right as you move down and zero rows are at the bottom. In <strong>reduced row echelon form (RREF)</strong>, pivots are 1 and are the only nonzero entries in their columns.</p><div class="math">[1  2 | 5]<br>[2 −1 | 0]<br>R₂ ← R₂ − 2R₁ → [0 −5 | −10]</div><p>Normalize the second row, then eliminate the 2 above its pivot. You obtain x = 1 and y = 2. Different valid operation sequences lead to the same RREF.</p><h3>Use the tools thoughtfully</h3><p>The playground stores exact fractions, supports undo, and can suggest one next step. Try a move yourself before asking for the next step. A row [0 0 | 1] means 0 = 1, so the system is inconsistent.</p>`,[
q('Protect the solution set.','Which operation is invalid?',['Multiply a row by zero.','Swap two rows.','Add twice row 1 to row 2.'],['Exactly. It erases an equation and can introduce extra solutions.','Swapping equations preserves all their solutions.','Adding a multiple of a different equation is reversible.'],'A legal row operation must be reversible.'),
q('Read an augmented row.','What does [0 0 | 3] tell you about a system in x and y?',['There is no solution.','Every x and y solve the system.','x = 0 and y = 3.'],['Yes. The row asserts 0x + 0y = 3, which is impossible.','That would be the equation [0 0 | 0], not [0 0 | 3].','The last entry is the right-hand side, not a variable coefficient.'],'Translate the row back into an equation.')],{prereq:['matrices'],initial:{rows:[['1','2','5'],['2','-1','0']],history:[]}}),
solutions:lesson('Solution sets','02 · Systems & spaces','systems','One answer.<br>Infinitely many. Or none.','A linear system is a collection of constraints. Watch its solution set change as you move the equations.','Where do both equations hold?','A solution must lie on every constraint at once. Parallel distinct lines have no common point; coincident lines share infinitely many.','Compare all three system presets. Then change only the right-hand side of a singular system.',
`<p>For Ax = b, a <strong>solution</strong> is a vector satisfying every equation. In two variables, a nontrivial equation ax + by = c describes a line. Solving two equations means finding the intersection of their lines.</p><h3>Three possibilities</h3><ul><li><strong>Unique:</strong> two nonparallel lines intersect once.</li><li><strong>None:</strong> distinct parallel lines never meet.</li><li><strong>Infinitely many:</strong> coincident lines describe the same constraint.</li></ul><p>Degenerate equations need care: 0x + 0y = 0 imposes no constraint, while 0x + 0y = c with c ≠ 0 is impossible.</p><h3>Rank tells the general story</h3><div class="math">consistent ⇔ rank(A) = rank([A | b])<br>unique ⇔ consistent and rank(A) = n</div><p>Here n is the number of unknowns. A consistent system with rank less than n has free variables and infinitely many real solutions. The rule also applies to rectangular systems.</p><h3>Separate the matrix from the target</h3><p>The same singular A can give no solutions for one b and infinitely many for another. Singularity alone does not imply inconsistency. If x₀ is one solution, all solutions are x₀ + z for z in the null space of A.</p>`,[
q('Keep A fixed; change b.','A = [[1, 1], [2, 2]]. Which right-hand side makes the system inconsistent?',['b = (2, 5)','b = (2, 4)','b = (0, 0)'],['Yes. Twice the first equation would require the second right-hand side to be 4.','These are the same constraint, so there are infinitely many solutions.','A homogeneous system always includes the zero solution.'],'The second row of A is twice the first.'),
q('Count the free variables.','A consistent real system has 4 unknowns and rank 2. How many free variables are there?',['2','0','4'],['Correct. Nullity = number of unknowns − rank = 4 − 2.','Rank 4 would be needed to remove all free variables.','Two independent constraints remove two degrees of freedom.'],'Subtract the rank from the number of unknowns.')],{prereq:['elimination'],initial:{m:[1,1,1,-1],b:[3,1]}}),
'vector-spaces':lesson('Vector spaces','02 · Systems & spaces','closure','What makes a space<br>a vector space?','Explore sets that stay closed under addition and scaling—and sets that break those rules.','Try to escape the set','In a subspace of ℝ², adding any two members or multiplying any member by any real scalar must keep you inside. A few successful samples cannot prove this for every choice.','Compare y = x, y = x + 1, and y = x². Try scaling a nonzero member by zero or by two.',
`<p>A <strong>real vector space</strong> is a set equipped with addition and real scalar multiplication satisfying the vector-space axioms: closure, associativity and commutativity of addition, a zero vector and additive inverses, distributive laws, and compatible scalar multiplication.</p><h3>Vectors need not be geometric arrows</h3><p>Real coordinate tuples, polynomials of bounded degree, and real m × n matrices are vector spaces under their usual operations. In each case, addition and scaling obey the same structural rules.</p><h3>Test subsets using inherited operations</h3><p>For a nonempty subset of a known vector space, it is enough to check closure under addition and scalar multiplication. The other axioms are inherited. Such a subset is a <strong>subspace</strong>.</p><div class="math">u, v ∈ S ⇒ u + v ∈ S<br>u ∈ S, c ∈ ℝ ⇒ cu ∈ S</div><p>A line through the origin is a subspace of ℝ². The offset line y = x + 1 excludes zero and is not. The parabola y = x² contains zero but fails closure: (1, 1) + (1, 1) = (2, 2) is not on it.</p><h3>Evidence versus proof</h3><p>One counterexample disproves closure. Many successful numerical samples do not establish closure for all inputs. Use the set’s equation to justify the general case.</p>`,[
q('Is the zero vector enough?','The parabola y = x² contains (0, 0). Is it a subspace of ℝ²?',['No; it fails closure under addition.','Yes; containing zero is sufficient.','Yes; every smooth curve is a vector space.'],['Correct. (1, 1) is on the curve, but (1, 1) + (1, 1) = (2, 2) is not.','Containing zero is necessary but not sufficient. Closure is also required.','Smoothness does not imply the algebraic closure rules.'],'Add the point (1, 1) to itself.'),
q('Recognize a nongeometric vector space.','Which set is a real vector space under ordinary polynomial addition and real scaling?',['Polynomials of degree at most 2, including zero.','Polynomials of degree exactly 2.','Polynomials whose constant term is 1.'],['Yes. Addition and scaling cannot increase the degree above 2.','Multiplying by zero leaves this set because the zero polynomial is not degree exactly 2.','Scaling by zero changes the constant term to 0.'],'Check what happens when you multiply by zero.')],{prereq:['span'],initial:{family:'line',a:1,b:2,c:2}}),
dependence:lesson('Linear dependence','02 · Systems & spaces','coordinates','Find the redundancy.<br>Name the relationship.','Three vectors in the plane must be dependent. Make that statement concrete by finding coefficients that connect them.','Can the third vector add a new direction?','Put the original vectors in columns before solving for their dependence coefficients. Rows of an echelon form are not automatically relationships between the original vectors.','Keep the first two vectors independent. Change the third until w = 2u − v.',
`<p>A set is <strong>linearly dependent</strong> if a linear combination equals zero with coefficients that are not all zero. Equivalently, at least one vector can be expressed using the others. A set containing the zero vector is dependent.</p><div class="math">c₁v₁ + ⋯ + cₖvₖ = 0<br>with some cᵢ ≠ 0</div><h3>Use the original columns</h3><p>To find a relation among vectors, form A with those vectors as columns and solve Ac = 0. The unknowns c are coefficients, not coordinates of the original vectors. The null space of A contains all such relations.</p><h3>A worked relationship</h3><p>In ℝ³, let u = (1, 0, −1), v = (2, 1, 3), and w = (0, −1, −5). Direct substitution gives 2u − v − w = 0. Thus w = 2u − v. Verify each coordinate; do not infer this relationship just by reading the rows of a transformed matrix.</p><h3>Dimension limits independence</h3><p>More than n vectors in ℝⁿ are always dependent. In this playground, an independent pair u, v already spans ℝ², so every w is a combination of them. If that pair becomes dependent, some targets are unreachable and coefficient representations may be nonunique.</p>`,[
q('Verify the relationship directly.','u = (1, 0, −1), v = (2, 1, 3), w = (0, −1, −5). Which identity is correct?',['w = 2u − v','w = −v/5','w = u + v'],['Exactly: 2u − v = (0, −1, −5).','Check the first coordinate: −2/5 is not 0.','The sum is (3, 1, 2), not w.'],'Calculate each coordinate of 2u − v.'),
q('Track the unknowns.','If the columns of A are your vectors, what does a nonzero solution of Ac = 0 describe?',['Coefficients of a dependence relationship.','Proof that every original vector is zero.','The unique solution of Ax = b for every b.'],['Yes. The entries of c weight the original columns to sum to zero.','A nontrivial dependence can involve entirely nonzero vectors.','A nonzero null-space vector implies the columns are not independent.'],'Expand Ac as a weighted sum of columns.')],{prereq:['span','elimination'],initial:{u:[1,0],v:[0,1],w:[1,1]}}),
basis:lesson('Basis & coordinates','02 · Systems & spaces','coordinates','Same vector.<br>A different address.','Choose two basis vectors, then describe a target using their coordinates. Change the basis while keeping the target fixed.','Give the target a new address','A basis must span the space and be independent. Its coordinates are unique; merely spanning is not enough for uniqueness.','Keep the target at (2, 1). Find a nonstandard basis in which its coordinates are (1, 1).',
`<p>A <strong>basis</strong> is a linearly independent spanning set. Every vector in the space has exactly one coordinate tuple relative to that ordered basis.</p><div class="math">w = c₁b₁ + c₂b₂<br>Bc = w, where B = [b₁ b₂]</div><h3>Coordinates depend on a basis</h3><p>For the standard basis e₁ = (1, 0), e₂ = (0, 1), the vector (2, 1) has coordinates (2, 1). For b₁ = (1, 0), b₂ = (1, 1), it has coordinates (1, 1), because b₁ + b₂ = (2, 1). The geometric vector has not changed.</p><h3>Why independence matters</h3><p>If basis candidates are dependent, some targets have no representation and reachable targets have nonunique representations. An invertible square basis matrix B makes c = B⁻¹w unique for every w.</p><h3>Dimension is invariant</h3><p>All bases of the same finite-dimensional space contain the same number of vectors. That number is its dimension. ℝ² has dimension 2; a line through the origin has dimension 1; the zero subspace has dimension 0 and the empty basis.</p><p>The playground uses column vectors throughout. Reordering a basis changes the order of its coordinates, so always state the ordered basis.</p>`,[
q('Read a new address.','With b₁ = (1, 0) and b₂ = (1, 1), what are the basis coordinates of w = (3, 2)?',['(1, 2)','(3, 2)','(2, 1)'],['Correct. 1(1, 0) + 2(1, 1) = (3, 2).','Those are its standard coordinates, not its coordinates in this basis.','That combination gives (3, 1), not (3, 2).'],'Solve c₁ + c₂ = 3 and c₂ = 2.'),
q('Spanning is only half the definition.','Three vectors span ℝ². Can that three-vector set be a basis of ℝ²?',['No; it must be dependent.','Yes; spanning is sufficient.','Only if all three vectors are nonzero.'],['Exactly. A basis of ℝ² contains two independent vectors.','A basis must be independent as well as spanning.','Even three nonzero vectors in ℝ² are necessarily dependent.'],'Compare the number of vectors with the dimension.')],{prereq:['dependence'],initial:{u:[1,0],v:[0,1],w:[2,1]}}),
subspaces:lesson('Subspaces','02 · Systems & spaces','closure','Inside the space.<br>Still a space.','A subset can inherit the structure of its parent space. Try to find the operation that makes a candidate fail.','Look for one counterexample','A subspace must include zero and be closed under every real linear combination. An affine line can resemble a subspace while missing the crucial origin.','Choose the parabola. Find a nonzero u for which 2u leaves the set.',
`<p>A <strong>subspace</strong> of V is a subset that is itself a vector space using the same operations. For a nonempty subset S, the compact test is: au + bv belongs to S for every u, v in S and every real a, b.</p><h3>Common examples</h3><p>The span of any collection of vectors is a subspace. Null spaces, column spaces, row spaces, and intersections of subspaces are subspaces. A linear equation with a nonzero right-hand side generally describes an affine set instead.</p><div class="math">x + y = 0 → subspace<br>x + y = 1 → not a subspace</div><h3>Zero is necessary, not sufficient</h3><p>A union of two different lines through zero contains zero but need not be closed under addition. Taking one vector from each line can give a point on neither line. Their sum as subspaces, consisting of all pairwise vector sums, is a subspace.</p><h3>Prove or disprove</h3><p>To disprove a subspace claim, give one failed requirement and a concrete counterexample. To prove closure, use arbitrary members and scalars, rather than just a few plotted points.</p><p>The playground classifies the three displayed families from their equations. Its individual sample checks are illustrations of those rules, not a substitute for proof.</p>`,[
q('Distinguish affine from linear.','Which subset of ℝ² is a subspace?',['{(x, y): x + y = 0}','{(x, y): x + y = 1}','{(x, y): x² + y² = 1}'],['Yes. Sums and scalar multiples preserve the homogeneous equation.','The zero vector does not satisfy this equation.','The unit circle does not contain zero and is not closed under scaling.'],'Check zero, then a general linear combination.'),
q('Combine subspaces carefully.','What is always a subspace when U and V are subspaces of the same space?',['The intersection U ∩ V.','The union U ∪ V.','The points of U whose norm is exactly 1.'],['Correct. A linear combination of members of both remains in both.','The union of distinct lines through zero usually fails addition closure.','The zero vector has norm 0, so that set excludes it.'],'A point in the intersection must satisfy both sets of closure rules.')],{prereq:['vector-spaces','basis'],initial:{family:'offset',a:1,b:2,c:2}}),
homogeneous:lesson('Homogeneous systems','02 · Systems & spaces','homogeneous','Zero on the right.<br>Structure on the left.','Every homogeneous system has at least one solution. Discover when it has more, and how its solutions fit together.','Find vectors that disappear','Ax = 0 always has x = 0. Nonzero solutions exist precisely when there are free variables: rank(A) is smaller than the number of columns.','Choose a rank-one matrix, then move along a nonzero solution direction.',
`<p>A system is <strong>homogeneous</strong> when its right-hand side is zero: Ax = 0. The zero vector is always a solution, so such a system is never inconsistent.</p><h3>When do nonzero solutions exist?</h3><div class="math">dim(null A) = n − rank(A)</div><p>If rank(A) = n, the only solution is zero. If rank is smaller than the number of unknowns n, nonzero solutions exist. For A = [[1, 2], [2, 4]], the equation x + 2y = 0 gives (x, y) = t(−2, 1).</p><h3>Solutions form a subspace</h3><p>If Au = Av = 0, then A(au + bv) = aAu + bAv = 0. Thus the solution set is closed under every linear combination and is the null space of A.</p><h3>Connect to nonhomogeneous systems</h3><p>If x₀ solves Ax = b, then every solution is x₀ + z, where Az = 0. The homogeneous system describes the directions you can move without changing the output. Unless b = 0, that translated solution set generally is not a subspace.</p><p>The playground uses unit-length basis directions for display, so its parameter t can differ from the parameter in an unnormalized expression such as t(−2, 1). Both parameterizations describe the same line.</p>`,[
q('Start with what is guaranteed.','Which statement holds for every homogeneous system Ax = 0?',['The zero vector is a solution.','There is a nonzero solution.','Every vector is a solution.'],['Yes. A multiplied by zero always produces zero.','An invertible A has only the zero solution.','Every vector solves it only when A is the zero map.'],'Evaluate A times the zero vector.'),
q('Count the invisible directions.','A has 5 columns and rank 3. What is the dimension of its homogeneous solution space?',['2','3','5'],['Correct. Rank–nullity gives 5 − 3 = 2.','Rank counts independent output directions, not null-space dimension.','Five is the dimension of the input space; three directions are constrained.'],'Use number of columns minus rank.')],{prereq:['solutions','subspaces'],initial:{m:[1,2,1,2],t:1,z:0}}),
'matrix-spaces':lesson('Spaces of matrices','02 · Systems & spaces','matrixspace','The vectors<br>can be matrices.','Treat each matrix as one vector-like object. Discover bases for symmetric, skew-symmetric, and trace-zero matrix spaces.','Build a matrix from its basis','The dimension counts independent parameters—not the number of rows or columns of each matrix.','Build a nonzero symmetric matrix whose trace is zero.',
`<p>The real m × n matrices form a vector space under entrywise addition and scalar multiplication. Its dimension is mn. A standard basis consists of matrices Eᵢⱼ with a 1 in one entry and zeros elsewhere.</p><h3>Symmetric 2 × 2 matrices</h3><div class="math">[[a, b], [b, c]]<br>= aE₁₁ + b(E₁₂ + E₂₁) + cE₂₂</div><p>There are three independent parameters, so this subspace has dimension 3. Symmetry is preserved under addition and scaling.</p><h3>Other structured subspaces</h3><p>A real skew-symmetric 2 × 2 matrix satisfies Aᵀ = −A and has the form [[0, −a], [a, 0]], giving dimension 1. Trace-zero matrices have the form [[a, b], [c, −a]], giving dimension 3.</p><h3>Some familiar sets are not subspaces</h3><p>Invertible matrices exclude zero and need not remain invertible under addition. Positive-definite matrices also exclude zero and fail closure under negative scaling. They have useful structure, but are not vector subspaces.</p><p>Our course uses real and rational arithmetic. Over other fields, some facts change: in characteristic 2, the distinction between symmetric and skew-symmetric needs special care.</p>`,[
q('Count independent entries.','What is the dimension of the real symmetric 2 × 2 matrices?',['3','2','4'],['Yes. Two diagonal entries and one shared off-diagonal entry determine the matrix.','The matrix has two rows, but that does not give its vector-space dimension.','Symmetry ties the two off-diagonal entries together.'],'Write the general matrix [[a, b], [b, c]].'),
q('Check closure before naming a subspace.','Do the invertible 2 × 2 matrices form a vector subspace?',['No; zero is excluded and addition can fail.','Yes; they all have the same shape.','Yes; each has rank 2.'],['Correct. I and −I are invertible, but their sum is zero.','A common shape is not enough to ensure closure.','Rank 2 is lost when some invertible matrices are added.'],'Try adding I and −I.')],{prereq:['vector-spaces','matrices'],initial:{family:'symmetric',a:1,b:1,c:2}}),
nullspace:lesson('Null & column spaces','02 · Systems & spaces','nullspace','What disappears?<br>What can be reached?','Look at a matrix as a map between two spaces. Its null space belongs to the input; its column space belongs to the output.','Connect inputs to reachable outputs','The null space lives in the domain and the column space in the codomain. They are shown in separate panels even when both spaces happen to be ℝ².','Find a nonzero input x that maps to zero. Then change A until only x = 0 does.',
`<p>For an m × n real matrix A, the <strong>null space</strong> is {x ∈ ℝⁿ : Ax = 0}. The <strong>column space</strong> is the span of A’s columns, a subspace of ℝᵐ. It contains exactly the reachable outputs.</p><div class="math">Ax = x₁a₁ + ⋯ + xₙaₙ<br>rank(A) + nullity(A) = n</div><h3>Read each space in the correct coordinates</h3><p>Input dimension n and output dimension m need not be equal. In this 2 × 2 playground they are equal, but the two panels represent distinct spaces. A kernel direction on the left collapses to the origin on the right.</p><h3>Find a basis carefully</h3><p>RREF reveals pivot columns and free variables. To obtain a basis for the original column space, take the corresponding <strong>columns of the original matrix</strong>. Row operations generally change the column space as a subset of the output space.</p><h3>Orthogonal complements</h3><p>For real matrices, null(A) is the orthogonal complement of the row space in ℝⁿ. The left null space null(Aᵀ) is the orthogonal complement of the column space in ℝᵐ. These are two different ambient spaces.</p><p>In the diagram, shaded regions indicate complete subspaces; the displayed basis directions are normalized for readability.</p>`,[
q('Choose the ambient space.','A is 3 × 5. Where do its null space and column space live?',['Null space in ℝ⁵; column space in ℝ³.','Null space in ℝ³; column space in ℝ⁵.','Both in ℝ⁵.'],['Correct. Five input coordinates produce three output coordinates.','This reverses the domain and codomain.','The outputs have three coordinates, not five.'],'Read Ax with x having one entry for each column.'),
q('Recover a column-space basis.','RREF has pivot columns 1 and 3. Which columns should you use as a basis for the original column space?',['Columns 1 and 3 of the original A.','Columns 1 and 3 of RREF(A).','Every nonzero row of RREF(A).'],['Exactly. The pivot indices identify independent original columns.','Row operations can change the actual column space.','Nonzero RREF rows span the row space, a different space.'],'Keep the pivot indices, but return to the original matrix.')],{prereq:['homogeneous','basis'],initial:{m:[1,2,1,2],u:[1,1]}}),
orthogonality:lesson('Orthogonality','03 · Geometry & approximation','orthogonal','A right angle.<br>A powerful simplification.','Use dot products to measure alignment. Then turn an independent pair into an orthogonal one.','When does the dot product vanish?','Zero dot product means orthogonality. The zero vector is orthogonal to every vector, but it cannot be part of an orthonormal basis.','Make two nonzero vectors orthogonal. Compare their dot product and angle.',
`<p>For real coordinate vectors, the <strong>dot product</strong> is u·v = Σuᵢvᵢ. The Euclidean norm is ‖u‖ = √(u·u). Two vectors are orthogonal when their dot product is zero.</p><div class="math">u·v = ‖u‖ ‖v‖ cos θ<br>cos θ = (u·v)/(‖u‖ ‖v‖)</div><p>The angle formula requires both vectors to be nonzero. A negative dot product indicates an obtuse angle; a positive one indicates an acute angle.</p><h3>Orthonormal means two things</h3><p>An orthonormal set contains mutually orthogonal, unit-length vectors. For a real matrix Q with orthonormal columns, QᵀQ = I. If Q is square, Q⁻¹ = Qᵀ and it preserves lengths and angles.</p><h3>Gram–Schmidt</h3><p>Start with a nonzero u. Subtract the projection of v onto u: w = v − projᵤ(v). Then u·w = 0. Normalize both nonzero vectors to obtain an orthonormal pair. If w is zero, the original pair was dependent and cannot yield two basis directions.</p><h3>Beyond real coordinates</h3><p>For complex vectors, the usual inner product conjugates one argument; simply summing products without conjugation is not the Hermitian inner product. This playground uses real vectors.</p>`,[
q('Compute before you eyeball it.','u = (2, 1), v = (−1, 2). What is u·v?',['0','4','−4'],['Yes. 2(−1) + 1(2) = 0, so these nonzero vectors are perpendicular.','The two products have opposite signs and cancel.','Add both coordinate products: −2 + 2.'],'Multiply corresponding coordinates, then add.'),
q('Know when Gram–Schmidt stops.','If v − projᵤ(v) = 0 with u nonzero, what follows?',['v is a scalar multiple of u.','u and v form an orthonormal basis.','v must be the zero vector.'],['Correct. All of v lies in span(u), so the pair is dependent.','There is no nonzero second direction left to normalize.','A nonzero scalar multiple also produces zero residual.'],'Rearrange the equation to v = projᵤ(v).')],{prereq:['basis'],initial:{u:[2,1],v:[1,1]}}),
projections:lesson('Projections','03 · Geometry & approximation','projection','The closest point.<br>The leftover direction.','Split a vector into a component along a line and a perpendicular residual. See why the projection is the closest point.','Drop a perpendicular to the line','For an orthogonal projection, the residual is perpendicular to the entire target subspace. A general oblique projection need not have this property.','Make the projection zero while keeping both input vectors nonzero.',
`<p>The <strong>orthogonal projection</strong> of u onto the line spanned by a nonzero v is the point p on that line closest to u.</p><div class="math">p = ((u·v)/(v·v)) v<br>r = u − p, with r·v = 0</div><h3>A worked example</h3><p>For u = (3, 1) and v = (1, 1), the coefficient is 4/2 = 2. The projection is p = (2, 2), and the residual is (1, −1). Its dot product with v is zero.</p><h3>Projection matrices</h3><p>P = vvᵀ/(vᵀv) satisfies P² = P and Pᵀ = P. Applying it a second time changes nothing. For a subspace with an orthonormal basis matrix Q, P = QQᵀ. For a full-column-rank basis B, P = B(BᵀB)⁻¹Bᵀ.</p><h3>Handle zero and distinguish oblique projections</h3><p>When v = 0, its span is the zero subspace, whose projection is always zero. The displayed line formula divides by v·v and cannot be used directly. An oblique projection is idempotent too, but generally not symmetric and not a nearest-point map.</p><p>The distance from u to the target line is the residual norm ‖r‖. Pythagoras gives ‖u‖² = ‖p‖² + ‖r‖² for orthogonal components.</p>`,[
q('Separate the component from the residual.','Project u = (3, 1) onto span((1, 1)). What is the projection?',['(2, 2)','(1, −1)','(3, 1)'],['Correct. ((3 + 1)/(1 + 1))(1, 1) = (2, 2).','That is the residual u − p, not the projection.','The input is not on the target line.'],'Find the coefficient (u·v)/(v·v).'),
q('Apply the map twice.','For an orthogonal projection matrix P, what is P²?',['P','I for every P','The zero matrix for every P'],['Yes. Once a vector is in the target subspace, projecting again leaves it there.','Only projection onto the whole space equals I.','Only projection onto the zero subspace is the zero map.'],'What happens when a point already lies in the target subspace?')],{prereq:['orthogonality','subspaces'],initial:{u:[3,1],v:[1,1]}}),
determinants:lesson('Determinants','04 · Structure & applications','determinant','Area changes.<br>Orientation tells a story.','Watch the unit square become a parallelogram. Its area measures a matrix’s determinant in absolute value.','What happens to one square unit?','The determinant can be negative; geometric area cannot. Its sign records orientation, while its absolute value records area scale.','Build a matrix with determinant −2. Keep the area scale positive.',
`<p>For A = [[a, b], [c, d]], <strong>det(A) = ad − bc</strong>. It is the signed area scale of the linear transformation. The columns of A form the edges of the transformed unit square.</p><div class="math">area after = |det(A)| × area before</div><h3>Sign, zero, and invertibility</h3><p>A positive determinant preserves orientation; a negative one reverses it. Zero means the columns are dependent and the plane collapses to a lower-dimensional set. A square matrix is invertible exactly when its determinant is nonzero.</p><h3>Useful identities</h3><ul><li>det(AB) = det(A)det(B).</li><li>det(Aᵀ) = det(A).</li><li>det(A⁻¹) = 1/det(A) when A is invertible.</li><li>Scaling an n × n matrix by c multiplies its determinant by cⁿ.</li></ul><h3>Row operations affect the determinant differently</h3><p>Swapping two rows negates it. Scaling one row by c scales it by c. Adding a multiple of a different row leaves it unchanged. These facts explain how elimination can compute a determinant.</p><p>The area interpretation generalizes to volume in higher dimensions. A determinant near zero can also signal numerical sensitivity, but a condition number is a more direct measure of that sensitivity.</p>`,[
q('Separate sign from size.','A matrix has determinant −2. What happens to a unit square?',['Its area becomes 2 and orientation reverses.','Its area becomes −2.','It must collapse to a line.'],['Exactly. Area scales by the absolute value, while the sign records orientation.','Geometric area is nonnegative.','Collapse requires determinant zero.'],'Use |det(A)| for area.'),
q('Scale an entire matrix.','For a 2 × 2 matrix A, what is det(3A)?',['9 det(A)','3 det(A)','det(A) + 3'],['Correct. Both column directions scale by 3, so area scales by 3².','That would describe scaling just one row or column by 3.','The determinant is multiplicative in scaling each row, not additive in this way.'],'Count how many rows are multiplied by 3.')],{prereq:['transformations','elimination'],initial:{m:[2,1,0,1]}}),
eigenvalues:lesson('Eigenvalues & eigenvectors','04 · Structure & applications','eigen','Some directions<br>stay on their line.','Find a nonzero vector whose image is a scalar multiple of itself. Compare stretching, reversal, collapse, and complex eigenvalues.','Search for an invariant direction','A negative eigenvalue reverses orientation along an eigenline. A zero eigenvalue sends its eigenvectors to zero. Neither requires the input eigenvector to be zero.','For the symmetric example, turn the candidate arrow until it lies on an eigenline.',
`<p>A nonzero vector v is an <strong>eigenvector</strong> of a square matrix A if Av = λv. The scalar λ is its eigenvalue. The line through v is invariant: the output remains on that line, or becomes zero.</p><h3>Interpret the scalar correctly</h3><p>If λ is negative, the output reverses orientation. If λ = 0, v belongs to the null space. The zero vector is never called an eigenvector, even though A0 = λ0 for every λ.</p><div class="math">det(A − λI) = 0<br>(A − λI)v = 0, v ≠ 0</div><h3>Diagonalization is conditional</h3><p>If an n × n matrix has n independent eigenvectors, A = PDP⁻¹. Not every matrix does. A repeated eigenvalue can have too few independent eigenvectors, as with [[1, 1], [0, 1]]. Real matrices can have complex eigenvalues; a 90° planar rotation has no real eigenvectors.</p><h3>Connect, but do not confuse, two geometries</h3><p>A real symmetric matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. For a general real matrix, the axes of the ellipse obtained from a circle are left singular directions, not necessarily eigenvectors. Singular values measure stretches and are nonnegative; eigenvalues can be negative or complex.</p><p>Explore all the presets, then explain why a rotation can still have complex eigenvectors even though the real diagram shows no invariant line.</p>`,[
q('Interpret a negative eigenvalue.','Av = −2v for nonzero v. What happens?',['The length doubles and orientation reverses.','The vector is not an eigenvector because its direction reverses.','The output length is negative.'],['Yes. The output remains on the same line; its orientation reverses.','A negative scalar multiple is fully allowed by the eigenvector definition.','Length is nonnegative and scales by |−2|.'],'Distinguish an invariant line from the orientation of an arrow.'),
q('Know the limits of diagonalization.','A 2 × 2 matrix has a repeated eigenvalue but only one independent eigenvector. Can it be diagonalized?',['No.','Yes, because it has a real eigenvalue.','Yes, because the eigenvalue occurs twice.'],['Correct. A two-dimensional eigenbasis is required.','A real eigenvalue alone does not provide enough independent eigenvectors.','Algebraic multiplicity does not guarantee an equally large eigenspace.'],'Count independent eigenvectors, not just repeated roots.')],{prereq:['determinants','nullspace'],initial:{m:[2,1,1,2],angle:0}}),
decompositions:lesson('Matrix decompositions','04 · Structure & applications','decomposition','One matrix.<br>Several useful factorizations.','Compare QR, pivoted LU, Cholesky, and eigendecomposition. Each makes a different part of the structure easier to use.','Choose a factorization for the job','A factorization comes with conditions and conventions. QR is broadly available; Cholesky requires symmetry and positive definiteness; real eigendecomposition requires a real eigenbasis.','Select Cholesky and find a symmetric positive-definite matrix.',
`<p>A <strong>matrix decomposition</strong> writes a matrix as a product with useful structure. The best choice depends on the problem, not on which factors look simplest.</p><h3>QR: orthogonal directions and triangular coordinates</h3><p>A = QR, where Q has orthonormal columns and R is upper triangular. In this square real playground, Q is orthogonal even for singular A, although R then has a zero diagonal entry. QR helps solve least-squares problems without forming AᵀA.</p><h3>Pivoted LU: elimination recorded</h3><p>PA = LU uses a row-permutation matrix P, a unit lower-triangular L, and an upper-triangular U. Pivoting avoids an unusable zero leading pivot and improves numerical behavior. Track P explicitly; forgetting it changes the claimed factorization.</p><h3>Cholesky: exploit positive definiteness</h3><p>A = LLᵀ for real symmetric positive-definite A. L is lower triangular with positive diagonal. A nonsymmetric or indefinite input does not meet the assumptions; the interface explains the failed condition instead of displaying invalid factors.</p><h3>Eigendecomposition</h3><p>A = PDP⁻¹ needs an eigenbasis. For real symmetric A, P can be orthogonal. For a general real matrix, P need not be orthogonal and real diagonalization can fail. The playground reports these cases.</p><p>Factor entries are displayed approximately. The reconstruction error uses the unrounded factors; it checks the identity, not the quality of a rounded display.</p>`,[
q('Choose a valid Cholesky input.','Which real matrix admits the standard positive-diagonal Cholesky factorization?',['[[2, 1], [1, 2]]','[[0, 1], [1, 0]]','[[1, 2], [0, 1]]'],['Yes. It is symmetric with positive leading principal minors 2 and 3.','This symmetric matrix is indefinite: its determinant is −1.','It is not symmetric.'],'For this 2 × 2 case, check symmetry, the top-left entry, and determinant.'),
q('Keep track of pivoting.','What identity does the displayed pivoted LU factorization satisfy?',['PA = LU','A = PLU in every convention','A = L + U'],['Correct. The displayed P applies row swaps to A before elimination.','Permutation conventions vary. Here P is defined by PA = LU.','LU is a matrix product, not a sum.'],'Read the permutation on the left of A.')],{prereq:['elimination','orthogonality','eigenvalues'],initial:{m:[2,1,1,2],method:'qr'}}),
svd:lesson('Singular value decomposition','04 · Structure & applications','svd','Turn. Stretch.<br>Turn again.','Decompose a matrix into orthogonal changes of coordinates and nonnegative stretches. Then decide how much structure to keep.','Keep the strongest directions','Orthogonal factors can rotate or reflect. Singular values are nonnegative and sorted; discarding one creates a controlled approximation rather than an exact factorization.','Keep just one singular value and compare the reconstruction error with the discarded singular value.',
`<p>Every real m × n matrix has a <strong>singular value decomposition</strong> A = UΣVᵀ. U and V are orthogonal in the full form; Σ is rectangular diagonal with nonnegative singular values in descending order.</p><h3>Read the action from right to left</h3><p>Vᵀ changes input coordinates, Σ stretches or collapses coordinate directions, and U changes output coordinates. Orthogonal transformations may include reflections, so “rotate–stretch–rotate” is only a shorthand.</p><h3>Full versus reduced factors</h3><p>For rank r, only the first r left singular vectors span the column space, and only the first r right singular vectors span the row space. Full U and V also contain directions in the corresponding orthogonal complements.</p><div class="math">Aₖ = Σᵢ₌₁ᵏ σᵢ uᵢ vᵢᵀ<br>‖A − Aₖ‖²_F = Σᵢ₌ₖ₊₁ σᵢ²</div><h3>Best low-rank approximation</h3><p>Keeping the largest k singular values gives a best rank-at-most-k approximation in Frobenius and spectral norms. For diag(3, 1), keeping one gives diag(3, 0), with Frobenius error 1.</p><h3>Link to PCA carefully</h3><p>PCA applies SVD to appropriately centered data. Uncentered SVD is still valid, but its strongest directions need not describe variance around the data mean. NumPy returns Vh, which is Vᵀ for real data; reconstruct with U @ diag(s) @ Vh.</p>`,[
q('Measure what was discarded.','A = diag(3, 1). Keep only its largest singular value. What is the Frobenius reconstruction error?',['1','3','4'],['Correct. Only the singular value 1 is discarded.','The value 3 is retained in the approximation.','The error is the square root of the sum of squared discarded singular values.'],'For one discarded value, √(1²) = 1.'),
q('Use the library convention.','NumPy returns U, s, Vh = np.linalg.svd(A). How do you reconstruct a real A with reduced factors?',['U @ np.diag(s) @ Vh','U @ np.diag(s) @ Vh.T','U + np.diag(s) + Vh'],['Yes. Vh is already Vᵀ for a real matrix.','Transposing Vh again uses the wrong factor.','SVD is a product of factors.'],'The h means conjugate transpose; for real matrices it is just transpose.')],{prereq:['decompositions'],initial:{m:[3,0,0,1],k:2,stage:3}}),
'least-squares':lesson('Least squares & distance','03 · Geometry & approximation','regression','When no line fits,<br>find the best compromise.','Fit five points with a straight line. See how residuals turn an overdetermined system into a minimization problem.','Minimize the sum of squared residuals','The plotted residuals are vertical differences in the data chart. The orthogonal projection interpretation lives in the five-dimensional observation space.','Adjust the slope and intercept—or compare the computed optimum—until the squared error is minimal.',
`<p>For an inconsistent system Ax ≈ b, <strong>least squares</strong> minimizes ‖Ax − b‖². For a line y ≈ mx + c, the unknown vector is (m, c) and each row of A is (xᵢ, 1).</p><div class="math">minimize Σᵢ(yᵢ − (mxᵢ + c))²<br>Aᵀ(Ax̂ − b) = 0</div><h3>Projection in observation space</h3><p>The fitted vector Ax̂ is the orthogonal projection of b onto col(A). The residual b − Ax̂ is perpendicular to that column space. With five observations, these vectors live in ℝ⁵, even though the data chart uses two axes.</p><h3>Normal equations and robust solvers</h3><p>If A has full column rank, AᵀA is invertible and x̂ = (AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀb. In numerical work, prefer QR or SVD-based least-squares solvers; forming AᵀA can square the condition number.</p><h3>Distance, error, and uniqueness</h3><p>The residual norm measures distance from b to the reachable output subspace. A squared-error value is its square. A best fitted output always exists; coefficient uniqueness requires independent columns.</p><p>This lab fixes x = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2. Their mean is zero, so the optimal intercept is the mean of y and the slope is Σxᵢyᵢ/Σxᵢ². Use the optimum as a comparison after trying your own fit.</p>`,[
q('Find the optimum for a tiny dataset.','At x = −1, 0, 1, the observations are y = 0, 1, 2. What is the least-squares line?',['y = x + 1','y = 1','y = 2x'],['Exactly. It passes through every observation, giving squared error zero.','That horizontal line has nonzero residuals at both ends.','That line predicts −2, 0, 2, so it misses the first two observations.'],'Check a line that fits the middle point and both endpoints.'),
q('Place the orthogonality in the right space.','For a least-squares solution x̂, what is perpendicular to col(A)?',['b − Ax̂','The coefficient vector x̂ in every case','Every point in the original 2D data chart'],['Yes. The normal equations say Aᵀ(b − Ax̂) = 0.','The coefficient vector lives in the input space and need not be perpendicular to anything here.','This is a relation among observation vectors, not all data-chart points.'],'Use the normal equations.')],{prereq:['projections','solutions'],initial:{ys:[-1,0,1,2,2],slope:0.5,intercept:1}}),
applications:lesson('PCA: put it all together','04 · Structure & applications','pca','A cloud of points.<br>One revealing direction.','Combine centering, projection, eigenvectors, and SVD to summarize a dataset. Make a prediction before finding the principal axis.','How much variation can one direction keep?','PCA measures variance around the mean. Turning off centering changes the objective to energy about the origin; that is a different analysis.','Keep one component and find a direction that captures as much centered variance as possible.',
`<p><strong>Principal component analysis (PCA)</strong> finds orthogonal directions that capture the most variance in a dataset. Here rows are observations and columns are features.</p><h3>Center before measuring variance</h3><p>Subtract the mean of each feature to form X. The sample covariance is C = XᵀX/(n − 1). Its eigenvectors give principal directions and its eigenvalues give variances along those directions.</p><div class="math">X = UΣVᵀ<br>principal directions = columns of V<br>explained varianceᵢ = σᵢ²/(n − 1)</div><h3>Project, reconstruct, compare</h3><p>For an orthonormal direction matrix Vₖ, scores are XVₖ and the centered reconstruction is XVₖVₖᵀ. Add the feature means back to reconstruct in the original coordinates. Explained variance ratio is the retained eigenvalue sum divided by the total.</p><h3>Important choices and limits</h3><p>Features with very different units may require scaling before PCA. A direction and its negative describe the same component. Equal eigenvalues mean there is no unique preferred axis within that eigenspace. If all centered observations are identical, total variance is zero and explained-variance ratios are undefined.</p><p>The playground shows one candidate direction and, for one component, orthogonal projection segments. Two components retain all 2D data. For the isotropic preset, all directions tie; “best” does not identify a unique line.</p><h3>Transfer the idea</h3><p>In image compression, a truncated SVD approximates a pixel matrix. In PCA, the matrix has a data interpretation and centering matters. Plain truncated SVD does not by itself solve missing-data recommendation problems.</p>`,[
q('Identify the missing preprocessing.','You want PCA directions of variance around the mean. What should you do before taking the SVD of the data matrix?',['Center each feature.','Add 1 to every observation.','Sort each column independently.'],['Correct. Centering makes squared distances relate to variation around the mean.','An arbitrary shift does not remove feature means.','Independent sorting changes relationships between observations.'],'Variance measures deviations from the mean.'),
q('Recognize a tied optimum.','A centered 2D dataset has covariance proportional to the identity. Which first principal direction is best?',['Every unit direction ties.','Only the positive x-axis.','Only the direction toward the original mean.'],['Yes. Variance is the same in all unit directions, so the first axis is not unique.','The x-axis is a valid choice but has no advantage.','The mean is removed, and isotropic covariance has no preferred axis.'],'Evaluate wᵀCw when C = cI and ‖w‖ = 1.')],{prereq:['svd','least-squares'],initial:{dataset:'tilted',angle:0,k:1,center:1}})
};
